## 2. Vertical Fourier Decomposition

Write the vertical elliptic torus as

\[
 E=\mathbb R^2/2\pi\mathbb Z^2
\]

with commuting fundamental vector fields `X,Y` adapted to the locked q79
vertical complex structure. Its complex generators are

\[
 Z={1\over2}(X-iY),\qquad \bar Z={1\over2}(X+iY).          \tag{2}
\]

The principal action is holomorphic, so `Z` is holomorphic, `bar Z` is
antiholomorphic, and pullback by `E` preserves bidegree, `partial` and
`barpartial`.

Every smooth form has a rapidly convergent vertical Fourier expansion

\[
 \alpha=\sum_{(m,n)\in\mathbb Z^2}\alpha_{m,n},          \tag{3}
\]

where the zero mode is exactly the torus average `Av(alpha)`. On a mode
`(m,n)`,

\[
 \mathcal L_Z\alpha_{m,n}=\lambda_{m,n}\alpha_{m,n},
 \qquad
 \mathcal L_{\bar Z}\alpha_{m,n}=\mu_{m,n}\alpha_{m,n}, \tag{4}
\]

with

\[
 \lambda_{m,n}={im+n\over2},\qquad
 \mu_{m,n}={im-n\over2},\qquad
 \lambda_{m,n}\mu_{m,n}=-{m^2+n^2\over4}.              \tag{5}
\]

Thus both eigenvalues are nonzero for every nonzero character.

## 3. Explicit Bott-Chern Contracting Homotopy

Suppose `alpha_(m,n)` has pure type and is both `partial`- and
`barpartial`-closed. The type components of Cartan's formula give

\[
 \mathcal L_Z=[\partial,\iota_Z],\qquad
 \mathcal L_{\bar Z}=[\bar\partial,\iota_{\bar Z}],
 \qquad [\bar\partial,\iota_Z]=0.                       \tag{6}
\]

For `(m,n) != (0,0)`, apply (6) twice:

\[
 \begin{aligned}
 \alpha_{m,n}
 &=\lambda_{m,n}^{-1}\partial\iota_Z\alpha_{m,n}\\
 &=(\lambda_{m,n}\mu_{m,n})^{-1}
   \partial\bar\partial\,
   \iota_{\bar Z}\iota_Z\alpha_{m,n}.                 \tag{7}
 \end{aligned}
\]

Hence every nonzero Fourier mode of a Bott-Chern cocycle is
`partial barpartial`-exact. Define

\[
 \mathcal H\alpha=
 \sum_{(m,n)\ne(0,0)}
 (\lambda_{m,n}\mu_{m,n})^{-1}
 \iota_{\bar Z}\iota_Z\alpha_{m,n}.                   \tag{8}
\]

Because the multiplier has magnitude `4/(m^2+n^2)` and smooth Fourier
coefficients decay faster than every polynomial, (8) converges smoothly. For
every Bott-Chern cocycle,

\[
 (1-\operatorname{Av})\alpha
   =\partial\bar\partial\mathcal H\alpha.               \tag{9}
\]

Equation (9) is an explicit chain homotopy, not a dimension-counting
assumption. It proves that averaging induces an isomorphism between full and
vertically invariant Bott-Chern cohomology. If an invariant cocycle has a
noninvariant primitive, averaging that primitive supplies an invariant one,
so injectivity of the inclusion follows as well as surjectivity.

